= Solution
{parent=c}

Keep the color matrix intact: $\mathcal A_\mu=A^a_\mu T^a$. Under <parity>, invariance of its contraction with the <vector current> requires
$$
\boxed{\mathcal A_\mu(x)\stackrel P\longmapsto\Lambda_\mu{}^\nu\mathcal A_\nu(x_P).}
$$
Under <charge conjugation>, the fermionic reordering in part (b) transposes the color matrix as well as the spinor matrix. For a fixed color matrix $M$, $\bar\psi M\gamma^\mu\psi$ becomes $-\bar\psi M^T\gamma^\mu\psi$. Hence invariance of the interaction requires
$$
\boxed{\mathcal A_\mu(x)\stackrel C\longmapsto-\mathcal A_\mu(x)^T.}
$$
This is not a rule that changes the sign of every individual color component: transposition also acts on the generators of the <fundamental representation>.

Write the <gauge field strength> as $\mathcal F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu\mathcal A_\nu-\partial_\nu\mathcal A_\mu+ig[\mathcal A_\mu,\mathcal A_\nu]$. Since $[A^T,B^T]=-[A,B]^T$, the <charge conjugation> rule gives $\mathcal F_{\mu\nu}\mapsto-\mathcal F_{\mu\nu}^T$. Including the coordinate reflection in <CP symmetry>,
$$
\boxed{\mathcal F_{\mu\nu}(x)\stackrel{CP}\longmapsto
-\Lambda_\mu{}^\alpha\Lambda_\nu{}^\beta\mathcal F_{\alpha\beta}(x_P)^T.}
$$
For generators normalized by $\operatorname{Tr}(T^aT^b)=\kappa\delta^{ab}$, the color contraction equals $\kappa^{-1}\operatorname{Tr}(\mathcal F_{\mu\nu}\mathcal F_{\rho\sigma})$. The two charge-conjugation minus signs cancel, and transposition reverses the order inside the trace without changing it. The <Levi-Civita symbol> acquires $\det\Lambda=-1$ under the remaining reflection. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L_\theta(x)\stackrel{CP}\longmapsto-\mathcal L_\theta(x_P).}
$$
**The <Yang-Mills theta term> is CP-odd; a generic fixed nonzero $\theta$ produces <CP violation>.** Setting $\theta=0$ removes this source. Although the density is a total derivative, it can affect the quantum theory through nontrivial topological sectors; the local transformation test is not rendered irrelevant by that fact.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

An experimentally observed example is **$K_L\to\pi^+\pi^-$**, a decay of the long-lived neutral <kaon> into a CP-even two-pion state. Without <CP violation>, the long-lived state would be CP-odd and this decay would be forbidden.

For the <CKM matrix> mechanism of <CP violation>, **three <quark generations> are the minimum**. Rephasings remove every complex phase for one or two generations, while three generations leave one physical <CP-violating phase>. This count concerns weak quark mixing. The <Yang-Mills theta term> in part (c) is a separate possible source of <CP violation> and does not require three generations.

